  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 342 AM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nWednesday looks to be another nice fall day with highs in the 70s \nacross the area and air at 825mb starts to dry out leading to mostly \nclear skies. Dry conditions will stick around for at least one more \nday as the 500mb ridge over the south central US builds into our \narea but temperatures should stay in the mid to upper 70s as clouds \nbuild in over our area ahead of the rain in the Central Plains. \n\nBy Thursday night into Friday morning the upper-level ridge \nbegins to move east while the surface high over Quebec continues to \nmove east increasing our chances for rain. The falling heights at \n500mb supports synoptic scale lift over strong southerly flow at the \nsurface supports moisture advection from the Gulf. However, models \nkeep the strong southerly flow off to our west on Friday so there is \nonly about a 30-50% chance that areas north of I-70 will see rain \nwith the best chances for rain staying NW. By Saturday, the strong \nsoutherly flow shifts east into our CWA and the chance of rain \nincreases throughout the day Saturday with areas west of I-35 seeing \nthe best chance (60-70%) for rain and decreasing chances (15-30%) \nfor areas south of a line from Kirksville to Butler. \n\nRain chances will stick around for the weekend as an omega blocking \npattern forms at 500mb with another low digging into the Pacific NW, \na ridge over the Great Lakes, and a strong low off of the east coast \nwith our CWA located in the trough portion of the omega block. This \nupper level setup coupled with the surface low sticking around to \nour west and the surface high to our NE keeping moist air advecting \ninto our area we will potentially see rain sticking around through \nSunday. Models have little to no instability present in our CWA for \nthe weekend so there is little concern for severe weather, and \nminimal chances for thunder. Models start to bring ridging back into \nour area by Monday which would bring an end to the rain, however the \nblocking pattern at 500mb may stick around longer than models \nsuggest which could keep rain around longer.\n\nIf the omega block does weaken by Monday, the 500mb ridge over the \nsouth central US will move into our area and could bring high \ntemperatures back into the mid 80s with a 45-60% chance that our \nsouthern most counties could see highs above 90 degrees.",
